owledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f658a318-42b0-511b-b456-f8782fa2ff34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285404} {:answer/id #uuid "15dbfcfe-475a-5c2e-918f-6a6f36b3b368" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285405} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d27925-46e3-5fab-81ed-7dd3554301bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285406} {:answer/id #uuid "45f9084b-8eba-58fb-9c77-f376633271d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285407} {:answer/id #uuid "7a4462af-e83e-5b61-be83-021ec8c564ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3d27925-46e3-5fab-81ed-7dd3554301bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285406} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee7cc81e-a46d-5c5c-97bd-25350773fc2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "821d7677-e14f-54cf-a344-d6f2cb89596c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106032" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26bd1293-8da9-5c2d-b283-aae2bfe221a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285411} {:answer/id #uuid "1404a4f8-234c-5235-9e86-442511cbcc32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285412} {:answer/id #uuid "48aff435-6b00-5e5c-93cb-877d30e224be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285413} {:answer/id #uuid "2282ca6a-2d5f-5dfa-bc02-69db07b3eff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285414} {:answer/id #uuid "56513223-11da-5186-bb4a-26bc1ad5f3b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26bd1293-8da9-5c2d-b283-aae2bfe221a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285411} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c19efc8-0644-5e8a-abcc-cd4e95d13467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7c4811d-0136-5556-a7aa-255ad6fca4d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-106291" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/3c07f3e732084327f02849cee9355a46ce7a1c5f905d509b1e1404915612c85c.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,-2)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(6,-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,-2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bfc5bb3-04ea-5e21-9cd9-c07da5511097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285418} {:answer/id #uuid "98810e9c-98ff-5a47-8f20-c096d8dfb45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285419} {:answer/id #uuid "bb085cfd-76cf-5a9c-880d-ba440ddfa49a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285420} {:answer/id #uuid "18df0079-cebb-5428-9e8d-a99004032963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285421} {:answer/id #uuid "d7febb20-21e6-5bfa-8632-63560adc382b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb085cfd-76cf-5a9c-880d-ba440ddfa49a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285420} :answer-field/id #uuid "57a9bc68-4e58-5c41-9a23-7c3233e37ee7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da600baa-a9d3-5724-9a5b-8920df8b8600" :question/math-academy-id "q-106293" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/d698646baac96e15db7f447c0e1af6dbc0cb03cae50084fbfbb6cab15eabf565.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=2.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/b76043921a3f9399049d6c3b4f557c7263da4d38a070c66a2dd444dbe2806c8e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a27d6b-22c3-579d-b3ec-42f2193f261a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285425} {:answer/id #uuid "eb18ed28-75b8-5cb9-9a2d-bcc8cac4816f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285426} {:answer/id #uuid "49ac2e7b-4a68-5a26-a6d2-ec6118fc43cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285427} {:answer/id #uuid "45eb8ebc-8ae6-5592-a165-3375aebf28cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285428} {:answer/id #uuid "6f40633a-bc89-5a92-990b-bf696c8fb8c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb18ed28-75b8-5cb9-9a2d-bcc8cac4816f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285426} :answer-field/id #uuid "db898e70-56f9-5772-9f9c-eb4a65e7b37b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9112bb74-acfb-590c-854b-03b69cc3e291" :question/math-academy-id "q-106294" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/eb1ef0ba48e294464496a901e6c593cb5e2bc215c688862e2b7eda3621f72469.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/6d73d4841b5e8ca1c3996d1996732b71245f2673e0e76a50a008600b95cfaf09.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the required semi-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "204541c0-24f8-58f7-85be-e4918313b591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285432} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd7c91a-9e6f-5864-9f46-854f138238f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285433} {:answer/id #uuid "b94103fa-ab6c-57be-bbb3-9db306bb275d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285434} {:answer/id #uuid "f796e756-fbad-5213-92fe-34e7bc9736b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285435} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0c64de-4799-5075-aeb1-e9e4bedcd99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "204541c0-24f8-58f7-85be-e4918313b591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285432} :answer-field/id #uuid "11b1e003-da9b-596c-ba40-c91af3644f87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d07f0ee1-8739-5256-bf30-c82b7eeddac5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106300" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a93ff279-b10b-53ce-97a8-65c746433fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285439} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bf81b9-2223-58e7-b783-70cabb89f670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285440} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb33231-df72-5ca8-8640-950b7f6b722b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285441} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e078bf-e8b9-5d98-a4bb-cdc4277ee5f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285442} {:answer/id #uuid "4920736c-ec67-5e91-ac7d-3d520193c11d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bb33231-df72-5ca8-8640-950b7f6b722b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285441} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b463a27-3a4a-5b59-93d3-df9bc4c770d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b4cc6ec-a5c3-5145-a122-c3b8c6352b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106693" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(5,-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(5,-3).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3660fd32-70e6-5f4c-bf98-27c86b4daeb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285446} {:answer/id #uuid "fc043568-6dad-5196-961f-1b7fa1355fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285447} {:answer/id #uuid "c6658429-805d-5158-878a-b40253eab0a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285448} {:answer/id #uuid "894e7701-f050-5718-bfa4-99ccd8b67b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285449} {:answer/id #uuid "71a7ab43-9280-5582-8056-6f4de3d648f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71a7ab43-9280-5582-8056-6f4de3d648f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285450} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa531025-2766-59b6-959b-7553463ec1fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "00d82038-4e0a-5079-b90e-fc5cfcb65e81" :question/math-academy-id "q-15845" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(x-1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(y-2) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(y-2)^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the Cartesian equation of the circle is $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7625393-d3a6-537a-82c0-4aa906791400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285453} {:answer/id #uuid "f69f25ed-e82b-551c-8aec-f8b85a84a806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f69f25ed-e82b-551c-8aec-f8b85a84a806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285454} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8764504-3b49-5c0c-b3d1-5d23409093c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "160bdb48-bc18-5f62-b990-4149f5312636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "160bdb48-bc18-5f62-b990-4149f5312636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285456} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ebb7b00-7da6-5922-973b-4b938af8ea33" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285455} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2a0a858-3d2c-54a9-9b6b-5ee29feb84c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2a0a858-3d2c-54a9-9b6b-5ee29feb84c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285458} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cd9d235-660d-5bbb-bb77-294dbe64aaaa" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a9d2783-5a0f-5b83-8cab-893c8a31f08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-275313" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=36.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daebd9d4-da86-5cf6-a677-5a05309ae2dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285461} {:answer/id #uuid "e662d6fb-9d1f-5288-b415-ab5ba9404968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e662d6fb-9d1f-5288-b415-ab5ba9404968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285462} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f616619-8511-5b23-8be9-c79aca45f14a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285460} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d32f64-ba33-5e3b-b3e3-60fafcc1b15c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41d32f64-ba33-5e3b-b3e3-60fafcc1b15c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285464} :answer-field/id #uuid "c91b0b0f-be2e-58d1-be3e-d427f43b2776" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69d3c50d-ab4b-52f2-a433-61eed1a36e8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-321890" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/b51c85930a5b93bcf7f3e7537cd39cf4134d93470b580c0f7276ddb58ade7297.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=4.$ Substituting $r=4$ gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ba2637c-f56a-5aad-9a68-50523954ad26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos(\\theta)-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ba2637c-f56a-5aad-9a68-50523954ad26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos(\\theta)-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285467} :answer-field/id #uuid "885b9121-05a0-59c5-bce9-25b0594a7877" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285466} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecf9ae45-8904-55d0-802e-38c3aef371cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sin(\\theta)+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecf9ae45-8904-55d0-802e-38c3aef371cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sin(\\theta)+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285469} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5bf5171-4f66-54fc-ac7f-1e659a08c462" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edaa1443-8d9e-5286-baa3-bc89f3671fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-321939" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/460b94dd8d68c7ed7e5bab45befb9c86496291ae7f192ab022e47493807525d5.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $5$ centered at $(-3,4)$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,4).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $5$ centered at the point $(-3,4).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd8ff7a2-0f61-525d-a304-ad1c14eb6d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285472} {:answer/id #uuid "9601c170-7b2e-5c66-9342-9a6a3b9dfb3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9601c170-7b2e-5c66-9342-9a6a3b9dfb3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285473} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2fedb1f-671d-57c8-aadc-46276312262d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "601ca41a-42dc-5d5a-9575-3ff7aa048d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "601ca41a-42dc-5d5a-9575-3ff7aa048d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285475} :answer-field/id #uuid "f063c384-0a90-5a58-b263-bace855ed6ae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f6fa581-0ad2-5257-8314-2e5550da3def" :question/math-academy-id "q-321948" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/ed6f528f7e2b873812d492cbeec386ab56af626a964e98b2929824ec9075d320.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/682f73ccec945de2789ecca71aa6dae650c485189c60ffc64ab7a7aa108665da.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e26fb45-f7d8-5be3-a5df-348f36b640f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e26fb45-f7d8-5be3-a5df-348f36b640f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285478} :answer-field/id #uuid "b98d5869-8856-5222-8f73-96204957f941" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285477} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "309921f1-1391-5cf9-8d3d-9df75fecd825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "309921f1-1391-5cf9-8d3d-9df75fecd825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285480} :answer-field/id #uuid "80ad2647-3d64-5745-98ba-408af04584d5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285479} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0a3f17c-7c15-5d72-8903-88097bc938ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0a3f17c-7c15-5d72-8903-88097bc938ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285482} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0976887-4faf-532f-a123-ca8a545a3158" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03d69679-f524-5057-8f75-9945b7cf6ef2" :question/math-academy-id "q-321989" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{7}{2},\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{11}{4},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{7}{2} & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x-\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{11}{4} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(y+\\frac{11}{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c72d4b7-bdb7-501b-a53f-79c953e7a862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285485} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c598f0-799f-5e78-bfd1-9da8d427099d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285486} {:answer/id #uuid "3c4b8896-8bd5-59d3-af12-5aec03c58d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285487} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa47a75-ff15-5d00-a43c-7ac86604a667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285488} {:answer/id #uuid "df31011a-9b55-5709-abad-15147cc41dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c72d4b7-bdb7-501b-a53f-79c953e7a862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285485} :answer-field/id #uuid "80db3e06-84ca-50cd-8c9d-7089ac04f59a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d41583ea-4992-5cc5-818e-760f3773a067" :question/math-academy-id "q-6878" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/de2b66f976dc204566516a1724fa19cc03bf8b8f4da1fa4f4a198fd656ad10ca.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $9$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(9,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=9.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3e320f-0bd9-57cb-8e7a-4067f894a141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=49\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=49\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285492} {:answer/id #uuid "e31b3788-ee9a-5c5b-b09f-075e81030297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285493} {:answer/id #uuid "396415de-b5c4-5a66-9d15-e4045a477adf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285494} {:answer/id #uuid "42556670-7adb-5b5b-8542-769e11453735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3.5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285495} {:answer/id #uuid "06b06e60-bfb0-506e-be8a-5c33dab0ec41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=14\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=14\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "396415de-b5c4-5a66-9d15-e4045a477adf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285494} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d7b030-14ac-50d3-bfef-30cf22f013e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3138b93-4b56-578f-8758-89f8193d13fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-6896" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $7$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=7.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86c4f1d7-805b-5a9e-adc4-f99d48079b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [0, \\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086767} {:answer/id #uuid "b517eaf0-d448-5440-9933-4a4e5231d3d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [0, 2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086768} {:answer/id #uuid "e22ec48c-a48b-582b-98e0-0c451cfb7b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [0, \\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086769} {:answer/id #uuid "f17614e4-8955-5095-98f0-7976720ae2cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [- \\pi , \\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086770} {:answer/id #uuid "347b457d-4b2d-5b60-85c9-6e93bd53eec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [- \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c4f1d7-805b-5a9e-adc4-f99d48079b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [0, \\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086767} :answer-field/id #uuid "c69c06b0-5b85-58f8-bf67-4a7baa78004e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9ceb55c-d1e9-5689-8032-0bd11852d402" :question/math-academy-id "q-78681" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/dd128890a13c0aa79d293b70063eeb8b78e07f56dd42774699d1b2f32e43dd0d.png)\n\nThe semi-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=7.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/25503e83f9a196cab4ded2fd93b09028b1608bd7d815edd538564e1aa611ca86.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the required semi-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86d4789d-0d1f-5075-889a-bbc9aa2c0bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285499} {:answer/id #uuid "362dbb24-f838-5556-96e5-ae67b7c32dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285500} {:answer/id #uuid "aa01087f-a623-5734-b3b0-13fcc9befc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285501} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4d4060-d2a9-5f59-9430-8b417435d1b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285502} {:answer/id #uuid "5538ccdb-55e4-58a5-8eed-8869e00e93af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86d4789d-0d1f-5075-889a-bbc9aa2c0bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285499} :answer-field/id #uuid "64a60757-8edf-5e5f-8e4f-57266d8bafd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "415c2174-2f13-595b-9668-7709d9fcceb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-79170" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/1407fa82fd00d07311cb34c6a3d5f0d0c79808d321d049d74114a6f4f2e5f6a6.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(3,2)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(3,2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40208fa8-350f-5d1a-bfb6-eb79193040b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285506} {:answer/id #uuid "dae8a998-a3e3-5155-9d91-acd1f198e8fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285507} {:answer/id #uuid "67666ee2-7893-5d32-9acd-bee7f015aae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285508} {:answer/id #uuid "a6bda015-db44-5c8f-918e-363ef8e23925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285509} {:answer/id #uuid "0e1ef3a4-aaa8-5bf5-b55f-bc2cf177e401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e1ef3a4-aaa8-5bf5-b55f-bc2cf177e401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285510} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b07eb1b-4626-5bc8-ab3d-16bab8d6e553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e74c508-de38-5c1f-966f-ae4879cf576c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79192" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/6b77b1b8a61b5ec7306fbc0b3d70618e7fdce2237bdcbfd017c23c01b80f2979.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-4,5)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(-1,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-4,5).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b22a3ccf-95a1-5e42-ae56-251a25a3e126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285513} {:answer/id #uuid "9a1ba595-03f3-5b76-aa2f-d2d15ded6142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285514} {:answer/id #uuid "e2eb507b-780a-5a44-b515-fd429e115ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285515} {:answer/id #uuid "80b00e7b-a374-5784-8dea-d1e04d4c65d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285516} {:answer/id #uuid "febd70cf-e59b-5da6-973f-162d74b42651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2eb507b-780a-5a44-b515-fd429e115ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285515} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ace54cc-89fa-5885-8314-306137ac2df5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "deee9493-d2a1-55ca-a638-e9ba7b8a8aab" :question/math-academy-id "q-79210" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/4d98583cd4066bc79695d9ceb75393d80c3a341e1a44c76a2144058da79bb5ca.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(5,-4).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/b71fec9561c150627ca6cfce06e8e79a9fa77f9395930f4bb9cf1a755cf350b2.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb737d9-6de8-5e97-8000-4ef442ee10f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285520} {:answer/id #uuid "26a22685-aa53-52bf-9042-affb9261b909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285521} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb053a0-af9a-58ad-953f-75f723563c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285522} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a500cb-13e8-5c35-94fb-220d40d5cd03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285523} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b156e2-d677-5816-8578-ade01ccf161a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb737d9-6de8-5e97-8000-4ef442ee10f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285520} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f557df-3792-5d35-9fc6-05c142f12f3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "480062d9-9533-5d48-bc7f-73b46822f0d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79221" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+3}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9.$"}]}
